# TODO: Empire 6

Plans for Empire 6 (`modules/i.empire.lbl`, `modules/i_emp.*.lbl`; the game's sources and notes are in `games/empire6/`). The rest of Image 128's list is `modules/TODO.md`.

- [ ] Empire 6 on a multiplexed Lt. Kernal (user, 11/22/2026; `empire` branch): when stations share the drive and more than one caller is playing, offer things that happen between them as they play. (0) First, and needed even without these: `e.empire6` is one REL file every station reads and writes (a player's save, combat rewriting the enemy's record, maintenance), so take an `&,13` lock around each read-modify-write (PROGRAMMING.md, "`&,13`"); likewise `e.empnews6`. (1) Who's playing: each station writes its player number (`g3`) and handle into a small shared file, record = station (port 0-15), cleared on leaving or at logoff, or ask `++ chatmode`'s planned quiet query (`&,16,5`, the entry above) who's online and match handles. (2) Realtime battles: attacking a player who is on now sends them a challenge, shown at their next prompt; the defender can answer within a time limit (send reserves, pay tribute, sue for peace, or ride out to joust, as the tourney does), each side's move through a shared exchange record under a lock; no answer in time, or the defender not on, and the battle is fought as now. (3) Chat: an Empire channel, `++ chatmode`'s room with only the players in it ("the war council"), from the game's menu; and one-line messages to one ruler (a taunt, a treaty offer) shown at their next prompt. (4) A herald: news as it happens; when a player on another station wins a quest, enrages Zircon or takes someone's land, a one-line notice reaches the other players at their next prompt, not only in the news file next call. (5) Live trading: offer another ruler who is on now grain, timber, spices or horses (the new stats) for gold or land; both have to accept before anything moves, under the lock, so neither can spend it twice. (6) The sysop joins in (user, 11/22/2026), at any station's console, as `++ chatmode` lets them into its room by turning `Cht` on: in the chat channel (3); in battles (2), taking a side live as one of the game's own figures: playing Lord Randall (the computer's ruler, `en$="RANDALL"`) when a player attacks him, or King George stepping in on a battle (reinforcements, a truce, or a ruling); and as the herald (4), announcing events of their own. (7) Watching a battle: other players on now follow a live battle as spectators, and can bet on it in Lucifer's Den (the Town). (8) Joint quests: two rulers on now go on a quest together (Empire II's quests, `i/emp.quests`), with better odds than alone and the reward shared. Testing needs two stations: see the Lt. Kernal in x128 entry above ("two x128s on one image file aren't a multiplexer")
- [ ] Empire 6: alliances and treaties (user, 11/22/2026; `empire` branch; for any BBS, players on different calls): two rulers sign a pact, no attacks on each other for N days, or a shared defence (an ally's soldiers come to your aid when you're attacked). Signing one takes a diplomat (`f%(6)`, which nothing uses yet). Breaking it costs nobles' loyalty and goes in the news ("<name> breaks the treaty!"). Combat checks the pact before an attack. Needs a pacts file (two players, the kind, until when)
- [ ] Empire 6: mail between rulers (user, 11/22/2026): offers, threats and treaty proposals left for another player, shown at the start of their next turn, with Accept/Refuse for an offer or a treaty (the alliances entry above)
- [ ] Empire 6: tribute and vassals (user, 11/22/2026): a ruler who loses badly can choose to become the winner's vassal rather than lose land, paying a share of their taxes to the winner each turn until they buy their freedom; shown on both rulers' status screens and in the news
- [ ] Empire 6: a market between players (user, 11/22/2026): sell orders that wait ("500 horses at $40") for another ruler to buy on a later call; the seller is paid at their next turn. Gives timber, spices, horses and cannons (fields 17-21, added for the quests) a use beyond the quests. Writes to the market file take an `&,13` lock on a multiplexed Lt. Kernal
- [ ] Empire 6: use the new stats (user, 11/22/2026): forts and cannons add to defence in combat, horses to attack, timber goes into building ships and palaces, spices are traded or keep serfs content (fewer leave or riot). Raids can aim at a store ("steal their spices"). Today `hs tb sp cn fo` are only won in quests and shown on the status screen
- [ ] Empire 6: spies and sabotage (user, 11/22/2026): pay to see another ruler's stats (today Y shows only your own), or to sabotage them (burn a mill, poison grain). Each attempt can fail, and a caught spy makes the news. Diplomats or a new "spies" count could carry it out. Scrying (user, 11/22/2026): a seer in the Town (a new item on its menu, beside Lucifer's Den and Utopia's Auction) gazes into a crystal ball, for a fee, at another ruler's realm: their stats as on Your Status, or a part of them for less (army, stores, buildings). No spy to lose, but the vision can be cloudy (a figure off by some percent, more for a cheap gazing or a ruler with many forts), and the seer might tell the other ruler who asked (in the news: "<name> was seen in the crystal")
- [ ] Empire 6: a royal treasury vault, and heists (user, 11/24/2026).
  - **The vault**: built (an investment, like a palace or a market) and raised in levels, a strongbox, an iron vault, a dwarf-forged vault, each holding more gold. Gold in the vault is safe from plunder when the palace is taken and from thieves; gold left out (`pn`) isn't, so a ruler chooses how much to keep at hand to spend. A little interest each year, and upkeep: guards (soldiers assigned to it), locks and traps, each raising its security
  - **Heists against you**: a thieves' guild tries the vault now and then (a random event, likelier the richer the vault and the lower its security). Foiled, or a share taken; caught thieves can be hanged, ransomed back to the guild, or turned into spies (the spies entry). In the news either way
  - **Heists by you**: a shady tavern in the Town (the Rat & Raven) where a crew can be hired against another ruler's vault. Plan it in a few choices, each with its odds against the target's vault level and guards: sneak in by night, tunnel under (timber), bribe a guard (gold), or strike while the court is at a festival or laughing at the jester (their entries). The loot, or the crew caught: the news names who sent them (a bounty on you, the bounties entry; an alliance broken, the treaties entry). The jester's gossip or a spy can scout the vault first
  - **Across the network** (that entry): a heist as an order resolved on the target's node, the loot sent back, and **a house's treasury** (the houses entry) in a vault of its own
  - Needs fields for the vault's level, its gold and its guards (record length: see the crops entry)
- [ ] Empire 6: sieges (user, 11/22/2026): an attack on a ruler with forts takes several of the attacker's turns; the defender sees "Under siege by <name>" at their next login and can send relief (soldiers, grain) or surrender. Needs a sieges file (attacker, defender, turns left). Planning them on a map: the siege planning entry below
- [ ] Empire 6: houses (user, 11/22/2026): players join one of two or three houses (teams), each with a treasury its members pay into and draw from, and a house rank in the news and on the wall
- [ ] Empire 6: barbarian invasions (user, 11/22/2026): now and then a horde (a strength figure) threatens every ruler; each player who sends soldiers wears it down, and the contributors share the reward when it's beaten. If it isn't beaten in time, every ruler loses land. Its progress shows at each login and in the news
- [ ] Empire 6: seasons and a winner (user, 11/22/2026): a season ends after N weeks or when a ruler reaches Emperor; the Hall of Fame records the winner (Empire II's `emp.fame`) and the game resets. This puts the config file's auto-reset settings to use (`ln`: on the land requirement, `mp`: on Emperor status; `e.emp 6 config`, games/empire6/variables.txt)
- [ ] Empire 6: a tournament ladder (user, 11/22/2026): the joust (`i/emp.tourney`) between real players, with challenges, standings posted on the Empire wall, and the champion in the news
- [ ] Empire 6: a royal tournament with jousting (user, 11/24/2026). Today's joust (`i/emp.tourney`, from Empire II) is one roll: a rival's wager, then one of 12 texts (`tourney 1.txt`-`tourney 12.txt`: win or lose 100 acres, 1,000 gold, a noble, grain, a shipyard, timber). Make the tournament an event the ruler hosts and plays:
  - **Hosting**: proclaim it a few turns ahead, at a cost (the lists built, prizes, a feast): the bigger the purse, the better the knights who come, and the more serfs attend (today too few serfs cancels it). It cheers the realm (unrest down, as a festival: that entry), and other rulers' champions can be invited (the ladder entry; across the network, that entry)
  - **A champion**: the ruler's knight, with skill that grows with each tournament, mounted on the realm's best horse (the `hs` stat: no horses, no joust), and a name and colours of the player's choosing
  - **The joust**: three passes against each opponent. Each pass, choose where to aim the lance (helm, shield, or low) and how to ride (charge hard, or steady): the opponent picks too, and the two choices with the champion's skill and horse decide it, a hit, a broken lance, or a knight unhorsed. Most points after three passes wins; unhorsed ends it at once. A bracket of four or eight knights, the final last
  - **The other lists**: archery (aim against the wind), and the melee on foot (the champion with a few soldiers), for prizes of their own
  - **The stakes**: the wagers of today's texts kept (a rival bets acres, gold, a noble...), now won or lost by how the joust goes; a champion can be hurt (out for a few turns) or, rarely, killed; a visiting knight beaten may swear loyalty (a noble gained, as the module already does)
  - **The court**: the jester mocks the loser (that entry), a suitor watching gives courtship points to a winning champion's ruler (the suitors entry), and the champion's victories go in the news and on the wall
  - Needs fields for the champion's skill, wins and health (record length: see the crops entry)
- [ ] Empire 6: bounties (user, 11/22/2026): a player posts BBS credits (`cr`) on a ruler's head, paid to whoever defeats them (in combat, or by taking their palace). Builds on the credit/money exchange in Empire 6's config (`en`, `nn`)
- [ ] Empire 6: crops and farmers (user, 11/22/2026): grow more than "grain". Now the harvest is one figure (i/emp.trading 111: `x=int((la*wc+rnd(1)*sf*wc+gr*wc)/3)`, capped at `(la+sf)*4`; `wc` the weather) and the people eat 5 bushels each (`sf*5+1`). Instead: crops the medieval world had (not potatoes, which reached Europe from the Americas only around the 1570s): wheat, rye, barley and oats (bread, ale, horse feed: horses, `hs`, eat oats), peas and beans, turnips and cabbages, onions, and cash crops, flax (linen) and hops (ale) and grapes (wine) to sell. Each takes acres you choose to plant, and does better or worse in different weather (rye and oats stand cold and wet, wheat wants a good summer; spreading the planting is the hedge against a bad year), with a rotation bonus for leaving land fallow or in beans. A new stat, farmers (taken from the serfs, as soldiers are): the harvest per acre rises with the farmers working it, so more farmers feed the people better, up to what the land needs; too few and crops rot in the fields. The record gets the new fields (as horses-forts did: i.empire's save/load, startup, combat's enemy copy, maintenance's editor; 200 bytes holds a few more numbers, then the length goes up again, the file remade by Reset Empire)
- [ ] Empire 6: a royal hunt in the forest (user, 11/24/2026), for meat to feed the kingdom in lean times. Today the people eat only grain, 5 bushels each a year (`sf*5+1`, i/emp.trading), so a bad harvest (the weather, `wc`) means hunger. Instead:
  - **The hunt**: the ruler rides out with horses (`hs`: more riders, a wider chase), hounds, nobles invited (their loyalty up) and serfs as beaters. The forest gives deer, boar, hare and pheasant; now and then a bear or wolves, a danger and a prize
  - **Meat**: a store of its own, counted in bushels' worth of food: the people eat it before grain, so a hunt stretches the grain in a lean year. Fresh meat spoils within a year unless salted and spiced (`sp`, spices: so spices feed the realm, not only trade)
  - **The forest**: part of the land, its game counted. Overhunting thins it, and it comes back over the years; cutting timber (`tb`) shrinks it too, so timber and game pull against each other. Royal forest law: poachers (hungry serfs, more in a famine) can be pardoned (loved) or punished (feared)
  - **Mishaps**: a noble gored by a boar, the ruler thrown from a horse (out a turn), a hunt that finds nothing in bad weather
  - **The court**: the hunt's best feeds a banquet (that entry: more guests, more courtship points, the suitors entry), the jester makes a joke of the one that got away (that entry), and a great kill (a bear, a white hart) goes in the news
  - Needs fields for the meat and the forest's game (record length: see the crops entry above)
- [ ] Empire 6: a royal fishing fleet (user, 11/24/2026), the sea's answer to the hunt (the entry above). Today ships (`yc`, built at shipyards, `f%(5)`) only carry serfs to colonies (50 a ship, i/emp.colony) and are lost at sea now and then (pirates, storms; nobles can save them; the wreck texts):
  - **The fleet**: fishing boats built at the shipyards, cheaper than ships, crewed by fishermen taken from the serfs (as soldiers are). The ruler sends them to the near grounds (safe, a small catch) or the far banks (herring and cod in plenty, but storms)
  - **Fish**: food, with the meat (the hunt's store): eaten before grain in a lean year. Fresh fish keeps only weeks: salted, smoked or dried it lasts the year (salt bought at a market, or spices, `sp`). Fast days made fish the food the Church asked for, so salted fish also sells well at the markets (that entry)
  - **The sea**: a good season and a poor one, as the harvest has its weather (`wc`); grounds fished too hard give less for a few years. Storms sink boats (the wreck texts again: survivors rescued are new serfs), pirates take catches unless a ship or two escorts the fleet, and now and then a sea monster story for the news
  - **A fishing village**: the boats need a harbour, so only realms with shipyards fish; a colony (`i8`) can have a fleet of its own, sending its catch home by ship
  - **The court**: a great catch (a sturgeon, a whale stranded on the shore) for a banquet and the news; the jester's fish jokes (that entry)
  - Needs fields for the boats, the fishermen and the fish (record length: see the crops entry)
- [ ] Empire 6: islands, as in Empire II (user, 11/24/2026). Empire II's ruler has three holdings, shown side by side, MAINLAND, ISLAND and FLEET (`mdl.emp.delx1.txt` 50575; census 51035): the island has its own grain, serfs, soldiers, horses and exports (`x(18)`, `x(20)`, `x(22)`, `x(24)`, `x(26)`, beside the mainland's 17-25), its own harvest (50725: from forts, island serfs and horses, and land), taxes ("King Midas Taxes Your Island") and famine ("Your Island Is Famished"), and you sail to it (`delx2` 50176), meeting other rulers' islands on the way ("First Mate Reports: ...'s Island On The Horizon": Attack or Continue, 50304-50318). Empire 6 kept only part of it, as the colony (`yg`, `ys`, `ye`; the island's soldiers and horses have no field: centipede-to-image.md, section 6).
  - **An island is nearly a realm of its own**: mirrored stats, the mainland's fields again (land, grain, serfs, soldiers, horses, timber, spices, forts, cannons, buildings, exports). So: a second REL record per ruler, laid out field for field as the first, so the same read, write and status code serves both with a record offset (records 2n-1 and 2n of `e.empire6`, or a file of its own, `e.emp6.isles`, by player number). This is the two-records case the Your Status entry plans for: there, MAINLAND and ISLAND (and FLEET) as columns at 80, as Empire II's census shows them
  - **Colony and island**: decide whether Empire 6's colony becomes the island (the colony's fields move into the island's record), or both stay (colonies as small outposts, the island as the second realm)
  - **Sailing**: ships carry grain, serfs, soldiers and horses between the mainland and the island; at sea, another ruler's island can be attacked (its forts and cannons defend it), or a storm or pirates met (the fishing fleet's sea, that entry)
  - **Exploring**: uncharted islands found on a voyage, Empire II's `isle1.txt`-`isle6.txt` (in games/empire6, not used by any module yet): treasure (90,000 gold), a volcano that damages the fleet, a lost colony that joins you (cannons, nobles, soldiers, serfs), and the rest
  - **The fleet** (Empire II's `s()`): ships at sea with what they carry, a third holding while a voyage lasts
  - Record length: the island record is the room the crops, hunt and fishing entries need too
- [ ] Empire 6: markets with goods (user, 11/22/2026): a market sells things, not only a sum (now each earns money from the serf count: i/emp.endturn 31, `p2=int(((rnd(1)*75)+sf/100)*f%(2))`). Kinds of goods: the crops above (flax, hops, wine), cloth and linen, salt, iron and tools (from the foundries), timber and spices (`tb`, `sp`), horses (`hs`), wool and leather; each with a price that moves with supply (what all the rulers hold) and the season. Buying goods you lack and selling your surplus, between turns; with the market between players (the entry above), other rulers' goods too
- [ ] Empire 6: Utopia's Auction between players (user, 11/22/2026): the Town's auction (i/emp.town 287-298) now sells you Utopia's own serfs at the price the game sets (`wb`). Expand it to auction serfs (and slaves, the Slave Market's 2,500) between rulers: a player puts some up with a reserve price, others bid on later calls (or live, on a multiplexed Lt. Kernal), and the highest bid when the auction closes takes them; Utopia keeps a cut, and still "hates greed" (her guards for a bid that's too low)
- [ ] Empire 6: the ruler's gender, courtship and marriage (user, 11/22/2026): ask a new ruler's gender, and use it for titles (the ranks, `d2$`: Lord or Lady, Duke or Duchess, King or Queen, Emperor or Empress) and in the texts (he/she; they for a ruler who'd rather). Courtship between rulers (players, or the game's own nobility for a ruler alone): gifts, letters (the mail between rulers entry), attending each other's festivals and banquets earn courtship points; enough of them and a proposal can be made and accepted. A marriage joins two realms in an alliance (the treaties entry) and the dowry passes at the wedding: gold, land, grain or goods, set by the bride's or groom's family as agreed. Weddings in the news and on the wall; an heir later, perhaps. Needs fields for gender, spouse (player #) and courtship points (record length: see the crops entry)
- [ ] Empire 6: suitors from the game's own nobility (user, 11/24/2026), for the courtship and marriage entry: a list of unmarried NPC nobles of the gender opposite the ruler's (either list for a ruler who chose "they"), each flagged free, courted (by a ruler, with the leader's courtship points: rivals can try), married (taken: the spouse's player number) or widowed (the spouse's kingdom fell or the ruler was weeded: free again after 30 days of mourning, with a share of the dowry). A suitor can die too, widowing the ruler (in the news), and a new one is dealt in. Dealt at each reset (`i/emp.maint`) by pairing a random given name with a random seat, about 12 of each gender, into a REL file `e.emp6.suitors` (drive 3); each has a trait for the texts and a dowry (gold, land, grain, goods). The name pools (24 ladies', 24 lords', 32 seats), the record and an example list: `games/empire6/suitors.md`
- [ ] Empire 6: a court wizard (user, 11/22/2026): hire one (a yearly wage, like the soldiers' pay) who casts spells, each with a cost (gold, spices, a rare reagent from a quest) and a chance to fail or backfire: bless the harvest (better weather for a year), ward off plague or rats, a fog over the army (an attack's odds), scry (the Town seer's spell, cheaper), curse a rival's grain store or granary, summon a storm on an enemy fleet. A rival's wizard can counter. Zircon (quest 9) could be the wizard's master: a wizard who's served long enough asks to study with him
- [ ] Empire 6: a court jester (user, 11/24/2026): hired at the court for a yearly wage (as the court wizard), with a name from a pool (Pip the Motley...). A joke at the start of each turn, the setup then the punchline, picked at random from `e.emp6.jokes` (a file the sysop adds to, as Image's sayings, `e.say`): a good one eases the serfs' and nobles' unrest. Cheeky jokes tease the ruler: now and then the ruler is offended ("Off with the fool's head?": dismissing the jester costs the court's mood, laughing it off wins loyalty). At banquets and festivals: courtship points with a suitor, or an envoy offended. Gossip: now and then a rumour about another ruler, true or not (the spies entry, cheap and unreliable). The plan, 12 jesters' names and 20 jokes to start (6 cheeky; each line at most 39 characters): `games/empire6/jester.md`
- [ ] Empire 6: festivals and banquets (user, 11/22/2026): hold a festival (a harvest feast, a saint's day, a tourney fair, the ruler's wedding) or a banquet, paid for in gold, grain, wine and spices: it raises the people's mood (fewer flee or riot, more immigrants: the census), the nobles' loyalty, and courtship points. Invite other rulers: on any BBS the invitation waits for them, and they attend on their next call (gifts exchanged, news); on a multiplexed Lt. Kernal, rulers on now come in person, with a live hall (the Empire chat channel), toasts, a joust for the guests, and the sysop as a guest of honor or as King George. Plenty of flavor text: menus (roast swan, venison pasties, eel pie, subtleties of sugar), minstrels and jesters, a guest who drinks too much or insults the host, a poisoned cup now and then (with a food taster to hire)
- [ ] Empire 6: siege planning (user, 11/22/2026; goes with the sieges entry): before attacking a ruler's palace, plan the siege on a map of their castle, in Commodore color graphics (C/G callers; a text map for ASCII): several castle maps (a motte-and-bailey, a concentric castle, a hilltop keep, a castle on a lake or by the sea), each with walls, towers, gates, a moat, a keep. Choose where to place the camp and the engines: trebuchets and mangonels (stones, flaming pitch, or diseased carcasses over the wall), battering rams under a shelter at the gate, siege towers rolled to a wall (the moat filled first, with timber and earth), scaling ladders, and sappers mining under a tower to bring it down (the defender can countermine). Also: cutting off the water or food and starving them out (days, against their grain store), a night attack, bribing a guard to open a postern gate, Greek fire or boiling oil on the defender's side, and the weather (rain stops fires and bogs down towers). The engines cost timber (`tb`), the attack horses and cannons (fields 17-21), and the defender's forts (`fo`) and wall repairs count against them. The siege plays out over the attacker's turns, shown on the map as it goes (breaches, fires, towers taken)
- [ ] Empire 6: Your Status (Y) in a module of its own, `sub.your status` (user, 11/24/2026), if the player file grows to two REL records per ruler (more stats than e.empire6's 200-byte record holds). `sub.*` modules load at 60000 (`gosub 74`), beside `i.empire` (3000) and the `i/emp.*` module at 4000, so 3071 (`sub.your_status`, which the other modules call by number: emp6-includes.lbl) stays as a stub that loads it and returns, and `i.empire` gets its 15 lines (3071-3085) back. The display it would take (11/24/2026): each stat put in `tt$()` with PRINT USING (`&,15,9`), `&,15,8` for the widest, one column at 40, two at 80 (`ll%`); more stats just make more rows. Cost: a load each time Y is pressed
- [ ] Empire 6: help for each prompt (user, 11/23/2026): at any prompt (the menu, Buy/Sell, Which investment?, Attack whom?, Give them how many?, the Town, the quests, maintenance) a help key shows what the prompt does and what its answers mean. The key: `?` already lists choices at several prompts (investments, attack, quests), so help could be `??` or `H`, or `?` everywhere with the list as its first part. One routine in i.empire (past the lines the modules jump to, as {:sub.plural}): `a$=<topic>:gosub {$33}2nn`, the topic a short name for the prompt (`invest`, `attack`, `feed`). Where the texts live, two ways: (1) separate SEQ files, `s.emp6.h.<topic>` on the System drive (`dr=1`, shown with `gosub 14`, as the quest texts are): simplest, each edited with any text editor and written to the disk from `disk/` by asm/build.sh (as `s.emp6.*` are), but each is a block or more on the disk (about 20 prompts: 20+ blocks; image128.d81 had 301 free after the quest texts) and a directory entry; (2) one REL file of help, `e.empire help`, indexed as `++ post` indexes the message bases (asm/post.asm): `&,16` finds the topic by binary search in its sorted index (`&,16,1` loads the index; a% the record), `&,16,8` reads the entry's chain of records, and Image's relread (`&,57`) shows it: one file, quick to find a topic, but it needs a tool to build the file and its index (a `tools/` script from a text file of topics, or a maintenance option to edit an entry), and `++ post` loaded (`a$="post":gosub 28`, at protostart, where no other overlay may be then). Start with (1); move to (2) if the files multiply or the disk gets tight. Either way, the texts written for 40 columns (wrapped to 39, as the quest texts are) or shown with word wrap
- [ ] Empire 6 across the network (user, 11/24/2026): rulers on different Image BBSes attacking, allying, trading and reading each other's news, over NetMail (NETWORK.md). The network is store-and-forward: a packet reaches another node hours or days later, on the next scheduled calls, so everything is an *order* resolved when it arrives (the Lt. Kernal items are the real-time side). A ruler is known across the network as handle@node (the node's 3-letter ID, `nn$`).
  - **The carrier**: a packet letter of Empire's own, `nm.K.<dest>/<origin>` (K for kingdom; M P F G B R U X E are taken), made by Empire into a queue on drive 3 and sent by `++ net` with the rest. `i/NM.sort` (or `i/NM.mail`) hands a received one to `e.empnet.in`, which Empire reads when the next ruler plays, or AutoMaint does. Each entry: a type, the version, from and to, the date, the fields, and a check value. The sysop turns it on and chooses the nodes it takes from, in `e.emp6 config` (as `nm.config` records 7 and 8 do for NetWall and Graffiti), and can block a node. Test with two nodes (NETWORK.md, "Testing with two nodes"; `tools/netpair.py`)
  - **Expeditions**: send soldiers, cannons and horses against a ruler on another node. They leave at once (subtracted then), arrive with the packet, and the battle is fought there against the defender as they are then; the result goes back (losses, land and gold taken, and those who came home). A longer route (more hops, from `nm.nodelist`) is a longer march: food for it, or deserters
  - **Alliances across nodes**: propose, accept, break, each a message; allies' help when one is attacked (troops sent ahead, as an expedition), a share of the spoils; a broken treaty in everyone's news
  - **Caravans**: grain, timber, spices or gold sent to an ally, or sold at another node's market (the market items); raided on the way, sometimes, more over more hops; escorts cost soldiers
  - **Envoys and spies**: an envoy carries terms (peace, tribute, a marriage, the courtship item); a spy brings back the target's stats as they were on arrival (the spies item, across nodes), or is caught and the target told who sent them
  - **Network news**: what Empire logs (`e.empnews6`: a new Emperor, a kingdom fallen, a great victory) sent to every node that takes it, tagged with its node, "On CIB: Lord X is now Emperor"
  - **A networked Empire wall**: wall entries go to the other nodes, as NetWall's do (`nm.G`), shown with handle@node
  - **Network rankings**: each node sends its top rulers weekly (as the weekly report goes to node 1, `nm.R`); node 1 makes a network-wide top ten and sends it back to all (as `nm.U.news`), and Empire shows it beside the board's own
  - **Events from node 1**: the administrator starts things for every node at once: a season and its reset on one date (the seasons item), a barbarian horde that moves from node to node until it's beaten, a plague, a festival with a prize for the network's best (the festivals item)
  - **Tribute and vassals across nodes**, and **bounties** posted network-wide (their items here, carried the same way)
  - **Fair play**: a node trusts what another sends, so each order is checked against limits on arrival (no army bigger than any ruler could have, dates in order, the version known), and each node keeps a log of what came and went, so a sysop can see who sent what
